  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe improve the initial construction by adopting a structured, periodic pattern to distribute 1's more evenly across the domain, reducing overlap at all lags. This contrasts with the initial naive half-ones approach. By leveraging symmetry and combinatorial spacing, we create a candidate that balances coverage while minimizing peak overlaps. The core challenge is nonconvexity, so we use a gradient-based optimizer with diverse initializations and parameter tuning, avoiding local traps through restarts and perturbations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- **Representation**: h(x) is a step function with n_points samples on [0, 2], h[i] \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- **Objective**: min\u2095 max_k [\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx] = max_{1\u2264m\u22642n_points-1} (corr[h, 1-h][m] * dx)\n- **Constraints**:\n  - Element-wise: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n  - Integral: \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- **Relaxation**: Use convex hull of possible h[i] configurations to ensure the optimizer stays feasible\n- **Solver**: SLSQP with adaptive step size and gradient regularization to handle nonconvex terrain\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parent validation:\n   - If initial_h_values provided, use it as base\n   - Ensure sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 through rescaling\nb. Initialization:\n   - Generate 5 variants: \n     1) Uniform spacing of 1's \n     2) Staggered shifted versions \n     3) Symmetric around 1.0 \n     4) Randomized with seed \n     5) From initial_h_values \nc. Core loop:\n   For each variant:\n   - Smoothen with Gaussian kernel to avoid sharp discontinuities\n   - Project to feasible space via constraint enforcement\n   - Run SLSQP with:\n     - Objective: max-correlation\n     - Bounds: [0,1] on all h[i]\n     - Constraints: integral = n_points/2\n     - Initial step size: 0.01\n     - Max iterations: 200\n     - Tolerance: 1e-6\nd. Perturbation phase:\n   - Apply small random noise (\u00b10.05) to all h[i]\n   - Re-scale to restore sum = n_points/2\n   - Repeat 3 times\ne. Final refinement:\n   - Select top 3 candidates from all variants\n   - Run L-BFGS-B with tighter tolerance (1e-8)\n   - Evaluate final objective with exact correlation computation\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   Default variants:\n   - Base: 200 samples with initial guess\n   - Symmetric: mirror first half to second half\n   - Uniform: spaced every 0.01 units\n   - Staggered: shift by 0.05, 0.1, 0.15\n   - Random: seed=42 variant\n   Adaptive adjustments:\n   - If stagnation detected (no improvement in 10 iterations), restart with new variant\n   - If correlation > 0.381, increase perturbation amplitude\n   - If sum deviation > 2%, apply clipping + rescaling\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Time allocation:\n   - 200s for parent validation and variant generation\n   - 300s for optimization: \n     - 100s for 5 variants with SLSQP\n     - 100s for perturbation phase\n     - 100s for final refinement\n   - 100s for final evaluation and fallback\n   Robustness measures:\n   - Always preserve a valid fallback from initial_h_values\n   - Clip all h[i] to [0,1] before rescaling\n   - Re-evaluate final candidate with exact correlation calculation\n   - If solver fails, return best candidate from previous iterations\n   - Track maximum correlation during optimization for early stopping\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   Final checks:\n   - Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] (clip and rescale if needed)\n   - Verify \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 exactly\n   - Re-calculate max_correlation with np.correlate\n   - Return (h.tolist(), final_c5, n_points)\n   Sanity checks:\n   - Check that all values are finite\n   - Verify that final_c5 is not NaN\n   - Confirm that the correlation calculation uses mode=\"full\"\n   - Measure execution time and ensure it stays under 600s\n   Fallback:\n   - If no valid solution found, return initial_h_values with its original c5",
